  "imageName": "arrow.triangle.branch",
  "intro": "Every Russian preposition requires a specific case - this is called 'government'. Some prepositions always take the same case (без always takes genitive). Others change meaning based on case: в + accusative means 'into', в + prepositional means 'in'. Learning preposition-case pairs is essential for speaking correctly!",
  "rules": [
    "Genitive prepositions: из (from), от (from person), до (until/to), без (without), для (for), у (at/possession).",
    "Dative prepositions: к (towards), по (along/according to).",
    "Accusative prepositions: в/на (into/onto - direction), через (through), за (behind - direction).",
    "Instrumental prepositions: с (with), за (behind - location), над (above), под (under), между (between).",
    "Prepositional: в/на (in/on - location), о/об (about)."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "Я иду в магазин. (I'm going to the store.), в + accusative (direction)",
    "Я в магазине. (I'm in the store.), в + prepositional (location)",
    "Книга на столе. (The book is on the table.), на + prepositional",
    "Положи на стол. (Put it on the table.), на + accusative"
  ],
